          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %em monoid
            one means a collection (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"}> set
            ) of elements equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
            (a “multiplication operation”) which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
            and has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+element"} unit element
          %p
            Hence monoid structure on a set is a fairly rudimentary form of algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlies
            many familiar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
            considered
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            (which are monoids with all
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+elements"} inverse elements
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            (which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            compatibly equipped with a
            %em second
            monoid structure).
          %p
            Therefore, in the algebraic literature monoids are, conversely, often called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                unital
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-groups"} semi-groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
            (cf.
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duoid"} duoid
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dioid"} dioid
          %p
            The terminology of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magmas"} magmas
            is meant to invoke this rudimemtary but foundational nature of basic algebraic structures: Monoids are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+magma"} unital
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+magmas"} associative magmas
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the notion of monoids is that of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            whose ubiquitous role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            (together with their
            %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#RelationBetweenAdjunctionsAndMonads"} associated
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#elementary_definition Elementary definition
          %p
            The classical definition of monoids in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+magma"} unital
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+magma"} associative magma
          %p
          #MonoidsInSets.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong monoid
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              equipped with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              if
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %strong>
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
                  ) a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
                  from the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                  of the set with itself to itself
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mtext -
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mtext -
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi M
                        %mo ×
                        %mi M
                        %mo →
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        (\text{-}) \cdot (\text{-})
                        \\,\colon\,
                        M \times M \to M
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %strong>
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+element"} neutral element
                  ) an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in M
            %p
              such that the following
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
              are satisfied
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %strong>
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
                  )
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi z
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ∈
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi M
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi z
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        x, y, z \,\in\, M
                        \\;\;\;\;\;
                        \\vdash
                        \\;\;\;\;\;
                        (x \cdot y) \cdot z
                        \\;=\;
                        x \cdot (y \cdot z)
                        \\mathrlap{\,,}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %strong>
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
                  )
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi M
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi x
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mn 1
                        %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        x \in M
                        \\;\;\;\;\;
                        \\vdash
                        \\;\;\;\;\;
                        1 \cdot x
                        \\;=\;
                        x
                        \\;=\;
                        x \cdot 1
                        \\mathrlap{\,.}
            %p
          %h3#inamonoidalcategory In a monoidal category
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoid in a monoidal category
          %h3#in_terms_of_string_diagrams In terms of string diagrams
          %p
            The data of a monoid may be written in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            as:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monoid data (for \"Monoid\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monoid-data-labeled.png"}/
          %p Thanks to the distinctive shapes, one can usually omit the labels:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monoid data, unlabeled (for \"Monoid\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monoid-data-unlabeled.png"}/
          %p
            The axioms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi η
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \cdot (\eta \otimes M) = 1_M = \mu \cdot (M \otimes \eta)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \cdot (M \otimes \mu) = \mu \cdot (\mu \otimes M)
            then appear as:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monoid axioms (for \"Monoid\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monoid-axioms-unlabeled.png"}/
          %h3#AsAOneObjectCategory As a one-object category
          %p
            Equivalently, and more efficiently, we may say that a (classical) monoid is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with a single
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          %p
            More tersely, one may say that a monoid
            %em is
            a category with a single object, or more precisely (to get the proper morphisms and
            = succeed "-morphisms)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            category with a single object. But taking this too literally may create conflicts in notation. To avoid this, for a given monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}M
            for the corresponding category with single object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            and with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo •
                    %mo ,
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = Hom_{\mathbf{B}M}(\bullet, \bullet)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphisms
          %p
            Similarly, a monoid in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes,I)
            may be defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object"} hom-object
            of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            with a single object, equipped with its composition and identity-assigning morphisms; and so on, as in the classical (i.e.
            = succeed "-enriched)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
