          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              A monoid in which every element has an inverse is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              = succeed "s." do
                %em semi-group
            %li
              The set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphisms
              of a given object in a category has a canonical monoid structure given by composition.
          %h2#remarks_on_notation Remarks on notation
          %p
            It can be important to distinguish between a
            = succeed "-tuply" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-tuply" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G \to \mathbf{E}G \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G = G//G
            is the action groupoid of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            acting on itself). On the left we crucially have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as a monoidal 0-category, on the right as a once-degenerate 1-category. Without this notation we cannot even
            %em write down
            the universal
            = succeed "-bundle!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Or take the important difference between group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representations
            and group 2-algebras, the former being functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G \to Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to Vect
          %p
            Or take an abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and a codomain like
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Vect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \to 2Vect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}A \to 2Vect
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}^2A \to 2Vect
                  \\,.
          %p
            All of these concepts are different, and useful. The first one is an object in the group 3-algebra of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}A
          %p
            Finally, writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            for the 1-object
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-monoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            makes notation behave nicely with respect to nerves, because then realization bars
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\cdot|
            simply commute with the
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathbf{B}G| = B|G|
          %p
            This behavior under nerves shows also that, generally, writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            gives the right intuition for what an expression means. For instance, what’s the “geometric” reason that a group representation is an arrow
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : \mathbf{B}G \to Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            which is
            = succeed "-associated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            = succeed "-bundle:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            the
            = succeed "-associated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  V//G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the vector space that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is representing on, and this is classified by the representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : \mathbf{B}G \to Vect
            in that this is the pullback of the universal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Vect
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi ρ
                        %mtd
                          %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V//G
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Vect_*
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}G
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\rho}{\to}&amp;
                  Vect
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            In summary, it is important to make people understand that groups can be identified with one-object groupoids. But next it is important to make clear that not everything that can be identified should be, for instance concerning the crucial difference between the category in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            lives and the 2-category in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            lives.
